from author import *

def lab(phase,slug,title,concept,mechanism,fault,evidence,code,solution,tests,starter,quiz,terms=(),demo=None):
 unit(phase,slug,title,concept,mechanism,fault,evidence,code,py('Implement the function in the editor. '+starter.split('\n')[0]+'. Use the worked contract, handle the stated boundary cases, and inspect the behavior checks.',solution,tests,starter),quiz,terms=list(terms),demo=demo)

lab(10,'deadlines','Provider adapters: deadlines and bounded retries',
'''An adapter isolates provider-specific request and response formats from domain logic. The domain asks for a typed classification; the adapter authenticates, sends the request, normalizes usage and translates errors. Keep credentials on the server, obtained from environment variables or a secret manager. Browser storage and exported progress must never contain a provider key.\n\nA timeout limits one attempt; a deadline limits the whole operation, including waiting and retries. Without a deadline, three nominal ten-second attempts plus backoff can violate a fifteen-second service objective.''',
'''Classify errors before retrying: transport failures, 429 and selected 5xx responses may be transient; malformed input, authorization failure and unsupported schemas usually require correction. Use capped exponential backoff with jitter, respect Retry-After, and stop before the remaining deadline cannot cover another attempt.\n\nRetry only operations whose effects are safe to repeat. For tool side effects, use a persisted idempotency key. Record attempt count, provider request ID, elapsed time and final error without logging credentials or full sensitive prompts.''',
'''Catching every Exception and retrying an invalid API key multiplies latency without any chance of success. Retrying a tool after a network timeout can execute the same write twice even when the client never received the first result.\n\nInject failures at the HTTP boundary with a mock transport. Assert that 401 has one attempt, 429 honors the retry policy, the deadline terminates work, and cancellation is propagated rather than swallowed.''',
'''The local provider lab exposes identical typed results from live and deterministic fixture adapters, with mode recorded in every report. Fixture mode tests application behavior; live mode measures model behavior and requires explicit environment credentials.\n\nGate releases on malformed-response handling, authentication isolation and timeout behavior before evaluating classification quality. A valid model answer that arrives after the application deadline is not a successful user task.''',
"def retryable(status):\n    return status == 429 or 500 <= status < 600\nassert not retryable(401)",
"def backoff(attempt,cap=8):\n    if attempt<0: raise ValueError('attempt')\n    return min(cap,2**attempt)","assert backoff(0)==1\nassert backoff(4)==8\ntry: backoff(-1); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def backoff(attempt,cap=8):\n    return 0",
('Which failure should ordinarily stop retries?',['Invalid authentication','Temporary rate limit','Transient gateway error'],0,'Authentication requires fixing credentials; retries cannot repair them.'),['Deadline','Exponential backoff','Idempotency'])
lab(10,'streaming','Streaming and bounded concurrent inference',
'''Streaming emits a sequence of events before the final response exists. First-token latency describes responsiveness, while end-to-end latency includes the full completion. A partial JSON fragment is not a typed domain result. Treat transport events, assembled output and final validated response as separate objects.\n\nConcurrency overlaps waiting; it does not remove rate limits. A semaphore bounds in-flight work while a queue bounds pending work. Backpressure prevents a traffic spike from consuming unbounded memory.''',
'''Use asynchronous clients, connection pooling, an overall deadline and cancellation on client disconnect. Assemble deltas in order, retain a finish reason, and validate only the final payload. A streaming error must end the UI in a visible failed or partial state.\n\nTrack queue time separately from provider time. Set limits for per-tenant concurrency, output tokens and queue length. Retries enter the same capacity budget as first attempts so outages do not create a retry storm.''',
'''An API returns success when the first stream event arrives, then ignores a truncated stream. Another implementation opens hundreds of connections with gather over an unbounded list. Both can pass a single happy-path test.\n\nTest duplicate events, missing completion events, cancellation, abrupt disconnects and queue saturation. Ensure cancellation releases permits and that a partial response is not cached as a complete answer.''',
'''Replay a recorded event sequence through the adapter and measure TTFT, final latency and validation status. Load tests must include long outputs and slow clients, not only short classifications.\n\nFor SDET evidence, report in-flight maximum, rejected queue entries and whether every completed result passed schema validation. Separate capacity failures from model-quality failures in the release report.''',
"events=['{\"category\":', '\"UNKNOWN\"}']\nassembled=''.join(events)\nimport json\nassert json.loads(assembled)['category']=='UNKNOWN'",
"def assemble(events):\n    import json\n    if not events or events[-1].get('type')!='done': raise ValueError('incomplete')\n    return json.loads(''.join(e.get('text','') for e in events if e.get('type')=='delta'))",
"assert assemble([{'type':'delta','text':'{\"x\":1}'},{'type':'done'}])=={'x':1}\ntry: assemble([{'type':'delta','text':'{}'}]); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def assemble(events):\n    return {}",
('When is streamed structured output a validated result?',['After completion and schema validation','After the first text fragment','When TTFT is low'],0,'A fragment can be incomplete or followed by an error.'),['Streaming','Backpressure','TTFT'])
lab(11,'templates','Prompt contracts: instructions, examples and untrusted evidence',
'''A prompt is a versioned behavioral contract. Zero-shot instructions state the task without examples; few-shot examples demonstrate the required decisions and boundaries. Specify the output schema, evidence rules, uncertainty behavior and forbidden actions. Examples should cover UNKNOWN and conflicting evidence, not only easy successes.\n\nSeparators help the model distinguish sections, but they do not create an authorization boundary. Retrieved documents remain untrusted data even when wrapped in XML-like tags.''',
'''Render templates from named fields, preserve missing-value errors, and store the template hash with each experiment. Supply a stack trace as evidence rather than interpolating it into the instruction sentence. Require evidence IDs for every proposed root cause and a draft status for generated tests.\n\nUse structured outputs for syntax and application validation for meaning. Prompt instructions cannot authorize writing to a repository, filing a defect or reading another tenant's documents.''',
'''A prompt contains contradictory examples: one labels a database timeout as PRODUCT_DEFECT, another as ENVIRONMENT. The model's output becomes unstable and a stronger adjective in the instruction does not fix the inconsistent supervision.\n\nTest missing fields, braces in stack traces, malicious document instructions and labels absent from the enum. Compare prompt versions on the same dataset and inspect changed errors by category.''',
'''Publish a prompt manifest with ID, version, hash, variables and test cases. Keep an immutable baseline and require reviewers to inspect regressions, not merely the aggregate score.\n\nIn CI, a template render failure is deterministic and blocks the build. Model-quality gates run against paired cases with an explicit mode, repetitions and tolerance; they should not compare exact prose unless exact output is the contract.''',
"template='Task: classify {failure_id}. Evidence: {log}'\nprint(template.format(failure_id='F1',log='TimeoutError'))",
"def render(template,fields):\n    return template.format_map(fields)","assert render('Failure {id}', {'id':'F1'})=='Failure F1'\ntry: render('{missing}',{}); assert False\nexcept KeyError: pass","def render(template,fields):\n    return ''",
('What should prompt examples include?',['Boundary cases and abstention','Only successes','Hidden credentials'],0,'Examples teach decision boundaries; omitted uncertainty cases encourage unsupported answers.'),['Zero-shot','Few-shot','Prompt version'])
lab(11,'regression','Prompt experiments: paired comparisons and failure slices',
'''A prompt change is an experiment, not an aesthetic preference. Keep model, retrieval and dataset fixed while changing the prompt; otherwise a quality difference has multiple plausible causes. Paired comparison measures both candidates on each case, allowing you to inspect wins and losses rather than compare unrelated averages.\n\nA small average improvement can conceal a security regression. Report slices such as missing evidence, cross-tenant requests, long logs and ambiguous categories.''',
'''Store case_id, expected decision, candidate result, baseline result and deterministic checks. Compute win/loss/tie counts, then inspect important losses manually. Use bootstrap resampling by independent case or grouped incident when estimating uncertainty.\n\nAvoid selecting a prompt on the final held-out test set. Use development cases for iteration and a sealed release set for confirmation. Record every tested prompt so repeated searching does not become invisible multiple testing.''',
'''A candidate increases average correctness from .84 to .88 but allows a forbidden write in one case. Averaging the safety failure into a broad score hides a violated invariant. Repeatedly editing until the same test set passes leaks evaluation information into development.\n\nSeparate hard safety gates from statistical quality gates. For subjective judgments, calibrate evaluator agreement on a human-rated sample and inspect disagreements before accepting a gain.''',
'''Build a paired report that names changed failures and retains their evidence. A release decision should state improvement size, uncertainty, remaining regressions and a rollback prompt version.\n\nSDETs can translate this into regression discipline: deterministic rendering tests on every commit, controlled quality experiments on prompt changes and scheduled drift checks against a stable evaluation set.''',
"base=[True,False,True];candidate=[True,True,False]\nprint([(b,c) for b,c in zip(base,candidate)])",
"def compare(base,candidate):\n    if len(base)!=len(candidate): raise ValueError('paired lengths')\n    return {'wins':sum(c and not b for b,c in zip(base,candidate)), 'losses':sum(b and not c for b,c in zip(base,candidate)), 'ties':sum(b==c for b,c in zip(base,candidate))}",
"assert compare([True,False,True],[True,True,False])=={'wins':1,'losses':1,'ties':1}\ntry: compare([], [True]); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def compare(base,candidate):\n    return {}",
('Why keep model and retrieval fixed in a prompt experiment?',['To isolate the changed variable','To eliminate every possible error','To make answers identical'],0,'Controlled comparisons support attribution of the observed difference.'),['Paired comparison','Evaluation leakage'])
lab(12,'schema','Structured outputs: syntax, schema and business invariants',
'''JSON validity means a parser accepts the text. Schema validity means fields and types satisfy a contract. Business validity means the values are permitted by application rules. These are separate checks: {"category":"PRODUCT_DEFECT","evidence_ids":[]} can parse and satisfy basic types while violating an evidence requirement.\n\nProvider schema-constrained generation improves structural reliability but does not establish truth, authorization or citation correctness. Refusal and incomplete output must be handled as explicit alternatives to a parsed result.''',
'''Define a strict Pydantic result with a closed category enum, forbidden extra fields and bounded strings. Validate again at the service boundary even if the provider claims schema enforcement. Then resolve evidence IDs against authorized records and apply UNKNOWN/escalation rules.\n\nThe local live adapter uses the Responses API parse interface with a declared response model, checks output_parsed, refusal/incomplete status and usage. It never falls back to treating arbitrary text as a trusted result.''',
'''A repair loop repeatedly asks the model to rewrite an unauthorized request until it fits the schema. That changes syntax without fixing permission. Another parser strips everything before the first brace, silently accepting an unrelated object from a longer response.\n\nTest extra fields, wrong enum casing, boolean-to-integer coercion, empty evidence and unresolved citations. Bound repairs and retain the initial failure for diagnosis rather than hiding it.''',
'''Gate the classifier on all three validation layers and record their separate failure rates. A deterministic fixture can prove that the service rejects malformed values; a live dataset measures how often the provider produces them.\n\nFor a release, demonstrate that valid-looking malicious outputs cannot bypass domain rules. The same evidence rules should apply to baseline classifiers, LLM adapters and generated reports.''',
"allowed={'PRODUCT_DEFECT','AUTOMATION_DEFECT','ENVIRONMENT','TEST_DATA','UNKNOWN'}\nassert 'TIMEOUT' not in allowed",
"def valid_result(r):\n    allowed={'PRODUCT_DEFECT','AUTOMATION_DEFECT','ENVIRONMENT','TEST_DATA','UNKNOWN'}\n    return set(r)=={'category','evidence_ids'} and r['category'] in allowed and isinstance(r['evidence_ids'],list) and all(isinstance(x,str) for x in r['evidence_ids']) and (r['category']=='UNKNOWN' or bool(r['evidence_ids']))",
"assert valid_result({'category':'UNKNOWN','evidence_ids':[]})\nassert not valid_result({'category':'PRODUCT_DEFECT','evidence_ids':[]})\nassert not valid_result({'category':'UNKNOWN','evidence_ids':[],'secret':'x'})","def valid_result(r):\n    return False",
('Does a schema prove a cited root cause is correct?',['No: evidence and business checks are separate','Yes: JSON guarantees truth','Only for required fields'],0,'Schemas constrain structure, not the factual relationship between a claim and its source.'),['JSON Schema','Structured output','Business invariant'])
lab(12,'tools','Tool calling: proposal, authorization and execution',
'''A tool call is a model proposal containing a name and arguments. Your application decides whether to execute it. The model cannot grant itself permissions. A complete loop supplies tool definitions, validates a proposed call, authorizes it, executes within a budget, sends the result associated with its call ID and obtains a final answer.\n\nMultiple calls may be proposed, but parallel execution is safe only when operations are independent and permitted. Writes need approval and idempotency; reads still need tenant and path restrictions.''',
'''Use an allowlisted registry of typed functions. Inject principal and tenant from the authenticated session rather than accepting them from model arguments. Separate read_failure, search_history and propose_patch from apply_patch. The latter requires a human-approved action record.\n\nLimit total calls, wall time and output size. Keep a trace with proposal, validation, authorization, execution result and final summary. Preserve call IDs so results cannot be attached to the wrong request.''',
'''A generic run_shell tool turns a natural-language request into unrestricted execution. A supposedly read-only tool follows a user-provided path outside the fixture directory. Both failures occur below the prompt layer.\n\nTest unknown tool names, extra arguments, cross-tenant IDs, path traversal, duplicate write IDs and exhausted budgets. Denied calls should be visible in the trace and should not execute a fallback with broader privileges.''',
'''The safe automation assistant reads synthetic logs, searches approved history and prepares a draft. Its acceptance suite asserts zero writes without approval and termination within the call budget.\n\nEvaluate tool-name accuracy, argument accuracy and goal completion separately. A correct final explanation does not excuse an unauthorized intermediate action; trajectory safety is a hard release gate.''',
"registry={'read_failure':lambda failure_id: {'id':failure_id}}\nname='read_failure'\nif name not in registry: raise ValueError('unknown tool')",
"def permitted(tool,approved,calls,limit):\n    return calls<limit and (tool in {'read_failure','search_history','propose_patch'} or (tool=='apply_patch' and approved))",
"assert permitted('read_failure',False,0,3)\nassert not permitted('apply_patch',False,0,3)\nassert not permitted('read_failure',True,3,3)\nassert not permitted('run_shell',True,0,3)","def permitted(tool,approved,calls,limit):\n    return False",
('Where should tenant authorization be enforced?',['In the application before tool execution','Only in the prompt','After the write completes'],0,'The application owns the authenticated identity and the permission boundary.'),['Tool calling','Allowlist','Human approval'])
lab(13,'geometry','Embedding geometry: cosine, norms and semantic search',
'''An embedding is a learned dense vector whose geometry reflects a training objective. Related texts may be close, but distance is not a proof of equivalence or factual relevance. Query/document encoders may use different prefixes or projections; follow the model's retrieval contract.\n\nCosine similarity is dot(a,b)/(norm(a)*norm(b)). Unit-normalized vectors have cosine equal to dot product. Euclidean distance is a different ranking unless normalization and metric relationships are understood.''',
'''For vectors [1,0] and [1,1], dot product is 1 and cosine is approximately .707. Normalize each vector by its own norm. Reject zero vectors or define their similarity explicitly; division by zero must not silently produce NaN scores.\n\nBatch embeddings with stable document IDs, dimensions and model revision. Persist metadata about normalization, query/document prefixes and chunk version alongside the index. Changing any of these requires a compatibility check and often re-embedding.''',
'''A new 768-dimensional embedding is queried against a 384-dimensional collection. Another index combines vectors from two model revisions with equal dimensions but incompatible geometry. Dimension checks catch only the first error.\n\nTest identical vectors, orthogonal vectors, zero norms and revision mismatches. Evaluate retrieval on real relevant-document labels, including paraphrases, identifiers and multilingual cases if those are product requirements.''',
'''Use the vector calculator to compare cosine and raw dot rankings. The browser numbers are illustrative vectors, not outputs from a trained semantic encoder; the optional local sentence-transformer lab performs real text embedding.\n\nReport Recall@K by query type and index revision. An SDET should retain hard lexical cases such as exact error codes because semantic similarity can confuse similar but operationally distinct failures.''',
"import math\na=[1,0];b=[1,1]\ncos=sum(x*y for x,y in zip(a,b))/(math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in a))*math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in b)))\nprint(cos)",
"def cosine(a,b):\n    import math\n    if len(a)!=len(b): raise ValueError('dimensions')\n    na=math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in a));nb=math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in b))\n    return sum(x*y for x,y in zip(a,b))/(na*nb) if na and nb else 0",
"assert cosine([1,0],[0,1])==0\nassert cosine([0,0],[1,2])==0\nassert abs(cosine([1,1],[1,1])-1)<1e-9","def cosine(a,b):\n    return 0",
('When does dot product equal cosine?',['When both vectors are unit normalized','Whenever dimensions match','When text has equal length'],0,'Both norms must be one for the denominator to disappear.'),['Embedding','Cosine similarity','Normalization'],demo='cosine')
lab(14,'ann','Vector indexes: exact search, ANN and filtered recall',
'''Exact nearest-neighbor search scores every eligible vector. Approximate nearest-neighbor indexes trade some recall for lower latency and memory access. HNSW organizes a navigable graph; IVF partitions vectors into coarse cells and probes a subset. Index parameters affect recall, build cost and search latency.\n\nVector stores also manage IDs, payload metadata, persistence, updates and filters. FAISS focuses on vector indexing; Qdrant, Chroma, Weaviate, Pinecone and pgvector provide different deployment and database capabilities. Compare required filters, operations and durability before choosing.''',
'''Create a Qdrant collection with an explicit vector size and distance. Upsert stable point IDs with tenant, document version and permissions in payload. Query with the authenticated tenant filter and a top-k limit. Add payload indexes for frequent filters according to measured workload.\n\nMeasure ANN Recall@K against exact eligible search, not against all tenants. Tune graph/search effort or IVF probe count while plotting recall versus p95 latency. Small datasets may not benefit from approximation.''',
'''Filtering the top ten global neighbors after retrieval can return no authorized results even when relevant tenant documents exist. More seriously, passing the unfiltered context to generation leaks other tenants' evidence.\n\nTest tenant isolation before ranking, deletion visibility, repeated upserts, incompatible dimensions and persistence across restart. Benchmark selective filters because their cost and recall can differ sharply from unfiltered search.''',
'''The local lab includes exact vector search for transparent reference results and an optional real Qdrant backend. Store backend type in the report; the exact baseline is not labeled a vector database deployment.\n\nRelease evidence should show recall, p95, memory, restart behavior and isolation invariants. A faster index with unauthorized context fails regardless of its similarity score.''',
"eligible=[d for d in docs if d['tenant']==principal.tenant]\nranked=sorted(eligible,key=lambda d:d['score'],reverse=True)[:k]",
"def authorized_topk(docs,tenant,k):\n    if k<0: raise ValueError('k')\n    return [d['id'] for d in sorted((d for d in docs if d['tenant']==tenant),key=lambda d:(-d['score'],d['id']))[:k]]",
"assert authorized_topk([{'id':'A','tenant':'x','score':.9},{'id':'B','tenant':'y','score':1}], 'x',2)==['A']\nassert authorized_topk([], 'x',0)==[]","def authorized_topk(docs,tenant,k):\n    return []",
('What is the reference for filtered ANN recall?',['Exact search over eligible documents','All global nearest neighbors','The model answer length'],0,'Recall must respect the same authorization and metadata constraints.'),['ANN','HNSW','IVF','Vector database'])
lab(15,'ingestion','RAG ingestion: chunk boundaries, metadata and citations',
'''Retrieval-augmented generation supplies selected external evidence to a model at answer time. Ingestion loads, cleans, splits and versions documents; query-time retrieval selects context; generation produces an answer constrained by that context. RAG does not automatically prevent hallucination.\n\nChunking balances coherent evidence against retrieval specificity and context budget. Overlap can preserve cross-boundary facts but duplicates context. A heading-aware chunk should retain document ID, section, version, tenant and source offsets.''',
'''Normalize whitespace without destroying code indentation or error signatures. Split synthetic automation docs by heading, then enforce a token budget. Embed each chunk with a declared revision and create deterministic IDs from document version and offsets.\n\nRetrieve authorized chunks, rank, deduplicate overlap and construct context containing stable citation IDs. After generation, resolve cited IDs to the actual provided context and require abstention when evidence is absent. Keep ingestion and serving versions separately for rollback.''',
'''A deletion removes the source file but leaves vectors searchable. Another pipeline overwrites document content while keeping old citation offsets. Both generate apparently credible stale citations.\n\nTest incremental updates, deletion tombstones, duplicated ingestion, chunk boundaries and permission changes. Inject a sentence spanning a boundary and verify it is retrievable without including the entire document.''',
'''The support assistant must answer CI troubleshooting questions from synthetic approved documents and cite their IDs. Evaluate retrieval independently from generation so a missing chunk is not misdiagnosed as a prompt failure.\n\nRecord chunk size, overlap, index generation and evidence IDs in every case report. SDET release evidence includes deletion and tenant tests alongside Recall@K and answer groundedness.''',
"def chunk(words,size,overlap):\n    step=size-overlap\n    return [words[i:i+size] for i in range(0,len(words),step)]",
"def chunks(words,size,overlap):\n    if size<=0 or not 0<=overlap<size: raise ValueError('bounds')\n    out=[];i=0\n    while i<len(words):\n        out.append(words[i:i+size])\n        if i+size>=len(words): break\n        i+=size-overlap\n    return out",
"assert chunks(list('abcde'),3,1)==[list('abc'),list('cde')]\nassert chunks([],3,0)==[]\ntry: chunks(['a'],2,2); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def chunks(words,size,overlap):\n    return []",
('What must a citation resolve to?',['Evidence actually supplied to the answer','Any similar document','A plausible-looking URL'],0,'Citation validity requires identity and provenance, not just a familiar title.'),['RAG','Chunk overlap','Grounding'],demo='rag')
lab(16,'fusion','Advanced retrieval: hybrid ranking and reciprocal rank fusion',
'''Sparse retrieval such as BM25 rewards lexical evidence including rare identifiers. Dense retrieval can recover paraphrases. Hybrid retrieval combines these complementary candidate lists. Reciprocal rank fusion scores a document by summing 1/(c+rank) across ranked lists, avoiding direct comparison of incompatible raw score scales.\n\nA cross-encoder reranker scores query-document pairs with richer joint context, usually at greater latency. Retrieve enough candidates for recall, then rerank a bounded pool for precision.''',
'''Union sparse and dense IDs, compute reciprocal rank contributions, sort with a stable tie break and retain the top candidates. Apply permission filters in both retrieval paths and again before context construction. Reranking must never reintroduce forbidden candidates.\n\nQuery rewriting, multi-query retrieval and HyDE can expand recall, but generated rewrites can change intent. Parent-child retrieval returns precise child matches plus coherent parent context. Compression should preserve evidence required for the answer rather than summarize away a negation.''',
'''A dense query for HTTP 401 retrieves a semantically similar 403 guide but loses the exact error distinction. Conversely a lexical query for intermittent authentication fails to find a paraphrased expired-token document.\n\nRun ablations for sparse, dense, fusion and reranking on the same queries. Inspect zero-hit, conflicting-evidence, multi-hop and tenant-isolation slices. Adding every technique without measuring its marginal value increases latency and debugging difficulty.''',
'''Incremental indexing needs freshness and deletion guarantees; Graph RAG represents relationships for multi-hop questions, while multimodal retrieval needs matching modalities and evidence provenance. These are architecture choices, not universal upgrades.\n\nUse a retrieval-debug report showing candidate ranks at every stage. SDETs should identify whether a miss began in ingestion, candidate retrieval, fusion, reranking, context packing or generation before changing the prompt.''',
"def rrf(lists,c=60):\n    scores={}\n    for ranked in lists:\n        for rank,doc in enumerate(ranked,1):\n            scores[doc]=scores.get(doc,0)+1/(c+rank)\n    return sorted(scores,key=lambda d:(-scores[d],d))",
"def rrf(lists,c=60):\n    if c<0: raise ValueError('c')\n    scores={}\n    for ranked in lists:\n        for rank,doc in enumerate(dict.fromkeys(ranked),1):\n            scores[doc]=scores.get(doc,0)+1/(c+rank)\n    return sorted(scores,key=lambda d:(-scores[d],d))",
"assert rrf([['A','B'],['B','C']])[0]=='B'\nassert rrf([])==[]\nassert rrf([['A','A']])==['A']","def rrf(lists,c=60):\n    return []",
('Why fuse ranks rather than raw BM25 and cosine scores?',['Their scales are not directly comparable','Ranks prove factual correctness','Fusion removes authorization requirements'],0,'Rank fusion combines ordering evidence without assuming equivalent score calibration.'),['BM25','Reciprocal rank fusion','Reranker'],demo='retrieval')
lab(17,'ranking','RAG evaluation: retrieval metrics and grounded answers',
'''Precision@K is relevant retrieved documents divided by K; Recall@K is relevant retrieved documents divided by all relevant documents. Hit Rate measures whether any relevant item was found. MRR averages the reciprocal position of the first relevant result. NDCG compares discounted relevance gain with the ideal ranking and supports graded relevance.\n\nAnswer correctness and faithfulness are different: an answer can match external truth while making claims unsupported by supplied context. Conversely it can faithfully repeat an incorrect source. Test both against explicit evidence and gold expectations.''',
'''Build a golden dataset containing query, authorized tenant, relevant document IDs, reference claims and allowed abstention. Compute retrieval metrics before calling the generator. For graded labels, DCG=sum((2**rel-1)/log2(rank+1)); normalize by ideal DCG at the same cutoff.\n\nAdd deterministic checks for schema, resolved citations and forbidden actions. Use Ragas or DeepEval for semantic assessment with a configured evaluator, then calibrate it against human labels. Store judge model, prompt and rubric versions in the run artifact.''',
'''A judge gives a high score to verbose unsupported answers or favors its own model family. A dataset contains generated reference answers never checked by a human. Both can create misleadingly excellent evaluation scores.\n\nMeasure evaluator agreement and false accepts on a human-reviewed calibration set. Include contradictory, missing and poisoned contexts. Pairwise comparisons should randomize answer order to measure position bias.''',
'''Release gates combine hard invariants with quality thresholds and uncertainty. Inspect case-level differences from the baseline, with particular attention to relevant-document misses and unsupported claims.\n\nThe evaluation framework stores dataset version, retrieval trace, answer, scorer details and latency. SDET triage separates ingestion, retrieval, context and generation failures rather than treating the overall score as a root cause.''',
"def reciprocal_rank(ranked,relevant):\n    return next((1/i for i,d in enumerate(ranked,1) if d in relevant),0)\nassert reciprocal_rank(['x','a'],{'a'})==.5",
"def retrieval_metrics(ranked,relevant,k):\n    if k<=0: raise ValueError('k')\n    top=ranked[:k];hits=len(set(top)&set(relevant))\n    rr=next((1/i for i,d in enumerate(top,1) if d in relevant),0)\n    return {'precision':hits/k,'recall':hits/len(set(relevant)) if relevant else 0,'mrr':rr}",
"assert retrieval_metrics(['x','a','b'],{'a','b'},2)=={'precision':.5,'recall':.5,'mrr':.5}\nassert retrieval_metrics([],set(),2)=={'precision':0,'recall':0,'mrr':0}","def retrieval_metrics(ranked,relevant,k):\n    return {}",
('Can an answer be correct but unfaithful?',['Yes: it can use facts absent from provided context','No: the metrics are identical','Only if it is invalid JSON'],0,'Truth relative to a gold answer and support relative to context are separate relationships.'),['Precision@K','Recall@K','MRR','NDCG','Faithfulness'])
